#@@BEGIN liquid_check_zip_passwords
#|#!/usr/bin/env python3
#|"""
#|Liquid ZIP Password Checker (Termux)
#|------------------------------------
#|Tells you whether a .zip file is password-protected (encrypted), how many
#|files inside are encrypted, and which encryption was used.
#|Optionally you can type the password you set to test that it is CORRECT.
#|
#|Note: a ZIP file never stores the password itself, so no program can read
#|the password back out of it - you can only check "is a password set?" and
#|"does this password work?".
#|
#|This version:
#|  * Shows a live progress bar + percentage while it checks every file
#|    ("Checking... [####------] 40% (4/10 files)").
#|  * Reports file sizes in MB/GB throughout, not just file counts:
#|      - total size of the ZIP
#|      - total size of the encrypted files
#|      - total size of the files the password actually unlocked
#|      - total size of any files that stayed locked
#|  * Tests the password against EVERY encrypted file (not just the
#|    smallest), so zips where different files have different passwords
#|    are detected.
#|  * Tries a few sensible variants of what you typed (trimmed, lower-case,
#|    capitalised) since Android keyboards auto-capitalise.
#|  * Tries UTF-8 and the legacy cp437 encoding for old ZipCrypto zips
#|    (matters for passwords with accents/symbols).
#|  * 5 tries, with a clear, final DONE / NOT DONE message - never quits
#|    silently.
#|  * Always ends with a liquid-glass HTML report (liquid_check_zip_passwords.html)
#|    and opens it. The password you type is NEVER written to that report.
#|    (--no-open = only save it, --no-report = skip it)
#|
#|First-time setup in Termux (once):
#|    pkg install python
#|    pip install pyzipper        (needed to test passwords on AES zips)
#|
#|Run:
#|    python ~/storage/downloads/liquid_check_zip_passwords.py
#|    python ~/storage/downloads/liquid_check_zip_passwords.py /path/to/file.zip
#|"""
#|
