#@@BEGIN liquid_set_zip_password
#|#!/usr/bin/env python3
#|"""
#|Liquid Set / Change ZIP Password - ONLY THE PASSWORD CHANGES (Termux)
#|====================================================================
#|Does ONE thing: puts a new password on a zip you already have.
#|
#|HOW (this is a byte-level copy, not a rebuild)
#|  The zip is copied byte for byte into a temp file. The only bytes that
#|  are replaced are the encrypted data of each file (re-encrypted with the
#|  new password, same AES strength as before; AES-256 if it had no password).
#|  Everything else is copied exactly as it was:
#|     headers, names, dates, attributes, extra fields, per-file comments,
#|     the zip comment, file order, data descriptors, folders, padding.
#|  Nothing is decompressed, nothing is recompressed, nothing is re-ordered.
#|
#|SIZE
#|  A zip that already has an AES password stays EXACTLY the same size.
#|  A zip with no password grows by 50 bytes per file (28 encryption + 11 +
#|  11 for the AES info block in the two headers). It never gets smaller.
#|
#|SAFETY
#|  * The ORIGINAL is only replaced at the very end, after every check passed.
#|  * If anything fails or looks wrong, it stops at once, deletes the temp
#|    file and your original stays untouched. This includes:
#|      - any file that is compressed (checked before AND while encrypting)
#|      - any file whose data does not match its recorded size
#|      - a damaged original (CRC / MAC mismatch)
#|      - the new zip failing any check afterwards
#|  * There is no "keep both" option: on success the new zip REPLACES the
#|    original (the zip file's own modified date is restored too).
#|
#|SETUP in Termux (once):
#|    pkg install python
#|    pip install pycryptodomex pyzipper
#|  (pycryptodomex = the AES engine, required.
#|   pyzipper = a second, independent library used only to double-check the
#|   result; the script still works without it but tells you it was skipped.)
#|
#|RUN:
#|    python ~/storage/downloads/liquid_liquid_set_zip_password.py
#|
#|REPORTS: besides the plain-text report next to the zip (<name>_report.txt), it
#|always ends with a liquid-glass HTML report (liquid_set_zip_password.html) and opens it.
#|Neither report ever contains a password.  --no-open = only save the HTML
#|report, --no-report = skip it.
#|
#|STORAGE: a full second copy is built next to the zip as <name>.zip.building,
#|then swapped in. Free space ends up the same if the zip already had an
#|AES password; adding a password makes the zip 50 bytes per file bigger.
#|"""
#|
